s compound  our hydrogen chloride is the fact that  it's these are called  hydrogen halides  so look it's very similar to having when  we react  a halogen with a metal we make a metal  halide  when we react hydrogen with a halogen we  get a hydrogen halide  now these hydrogen halides actually  dissolve in water  to make acidic solutions for example  2hcl this would dissolve in water to  form  hydrochloric acid which is a strong acid  so as you can see those first two types  of reactions are very very simple  the next type of reaction that halogens  we need to know about  involving halogens are displacement  reactions now all a displacement  reaction is  is when a more reactive element replaces  a less reactive element  so we're going to have a look at this  example here here we have chlorine  all by itself and we have potassium  bromide  so potassium and bromine in a compound  here  now if we have a look at our halogens  as you go basically at the top you have  your most reactive elements  and the further you go down your group  you get less and less reactive  so if we look at the two different  halogens here chlorine and bromine  we can see that chlorine is more  reactive than bromine  so because chlorine started by itself it  will be able to replace this bromine  because it's more reactive if it  replaces it so instead of now having  potassium bromide  i would now have potassium  chloride again look how it ends eyed now  it's in a compound  but that's not all the bromine has now  been replaced it's been kicked out  so now i just have the bromine  all by itself  so to quickly show you this is a symbol  equation underneath  it would look like this  kcl plus br2  and just to balance it i need a 2 there  and a 2 there  so remember a displacement reaction  is when a more reactive element  replaces a  less reactive element  and we can actually see this we can  actually show the reactivity  of the halogens by doing a series of  reactions  so what we have here is potassium  chloride solution which is a colorless  solution  potassium bromide solution which is  colored this solution  and potassium iodide solution what each  box represents  is a reaction between one of these  things  and one of these solutions so remember  if we've got displacement reaction we  must have a more reactive element  replacing a less reactive element  so let's go through and have a look if  we'd see any reactions so first of all  chlorine and chlorine so  the halogen here is chlorine the  hydrogen here is chlorine one  isn't more reactive than the other  they're the same level of reactivity  so we would not see a reaction here  this time we've got chlorine water  and the halogen is bromine so the  chlorine  would displace the bromine and we'd see  a change in color because bromine water  is a kind of orangey color this one here  if we react to chlorine water with  potassium iodide  the chlorine is more reactive than the  iodine it's higher  in group seven so it would displace the  iodine  and we'd actually get a brown solution  because iodine water is brown  so let's have a look at this one if we  reacted bromine water  with hydrogen oh sorry potassium  chloride  we could see that bromine is less  reactive than chlorine  so it would be not be able to displace  it  bromine and bromine have the same level  of reactivity  so we would not see a displacement  reaction but  bromine is more reactive than iodine  so the bromine would replace the iodine  and the iodine would be left in the  water to give a brown color  now finally if i added iodine to any of  these solutions  iodine isn't more reactive than chlorine  bromine or iodine  so none of this bottom row i would see a  reaction  so those are the three different types  of reactions  involving halogens thank you
 

